      <h1 class="ed-title">Treadmill Grinding Noise: Rollers, Bearings or Motor?</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        A grinding treadmill almost always has metal or grit rubbing where it should be rolling. If the grind is there with nobody on the belt and rises with speed, suspect a roller bearing or the motor; if it only appears when you walk, suspect a dry or worn deck or debris under the belt; if it only happens while the incline moves, it is the lift mechanism. Here is how to narrow it down, and when to stop using the machine.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 26, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on manufacturer manuals and service guidance, and the faults owners report most.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#short-answer" class="btn-ghost">Find the Grind</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          Grinding is the treadmill noise least likely to be harmless. A hum is the motor doing its job and a faint click
          can be a belt seam, but a rough, gritty or scraping sound usually means a bearing is breaking down, two parts are
          touching that should not, or the belt is dragging across a deck that has lost its lubricant. The good news is that
          the moment the grind appears tells you a lot about where it lives. You need a few minutes, the treadmill's manual,
          and the machine unplugged for every check that involves putting your hands near the belt, rollers or motor. This
          guide is general: designs differ by brand and model, so where a step depends on your machine (removing the motor
          cover, loosening the belt, lubricating) follow the manual. If the grind comes with a burning smell, a belt that
          stalls or jerks, or a machine that cuts out, read the section on when to stop using it before anything else.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="Jump to">
          <h2>Jump to</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#find-it">How to Pin Down Where the Grind Comes From</a></li>
            <li><a href="#rollers">Roller Bearings: Front and Rear</a></li>
            <li><a href="#motor">Motor Bearings and the Motor Itself</a></li>
            <li><a href="#drive-belt">Drive Belt and Pulleys</a></li>
            <li><a href="#debris">Debris Under the Running Belt</a></li>
            <li><a href="#deck">A Dry or Worn Deck and Belt</a></li>
            <li><a href="#incline">Grinding During Incline Changes</a></li>
            <li><a href="#start-stop">Grinding at Start-Up or When It Stops</a></li>
            <li><a href="#compare">Grinding vs Other Treadmill Noises</a></li>
            <li><a href="#stop">When to Stop Using the Treadmill</a></li>
            <li><a href="#warranty">Check the Warranty Before Any Repair</a></li>
            <li><a href="#repair">What a Repair Involves</a></li>
            <li><a href="#walking-pad">Grinding on a Walking Pad</a></li>
            <li><a href="#prevent">How to Keep It From Coming Back</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Run the treadmill empty, then walk on it, then change the incline, and note when the grind appears. A grind
            with nobody on that rises with belt speed is usually a roller bearing (front or rear) or a motor bearing. A grind
            only under your weight points to a dry or worn deck or grit under the belt. A grind only while the incline moves is
            the incline motor or its gear.</strong> Cleaning and lubricating fix the load-only cases; bearings and motors are
            parts jobs, so check the warranty first.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Empty belt, gets faster with speed:</strong> roller bearings, motor bearings, drive belt or pulley.</li>
            <li><strong>Only when someone walks:</strong> dry deck, worn deck or belt underside, debris under the belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Only during incline changes:</strong> incline motor, lift screw or gear, something trapped under the deck.</li>
            <li><strong>Only at start or stop:</strong> a bearing that is dry or worn, a slipping belt, or the drive belt.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">When it grinds</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Most likely source</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">First check</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Nobody on, rises with belt speed, from the rear</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Rear roller bearing</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Unplug; spin the rear roller by hand and feel for roughness</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Nobody on, rises with speed, from the front</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Front roller bearing, motor bearing, drive belt or pulley</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Unplug; look under the motor cover per the manual</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Only with someone walking or running</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dry or worn deck, grit under the belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Clean under the belt; lubricate if the manual says it is due</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Only while the incline moves</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline motor, lift screw or gear</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check under the deck for debris; run incline calibration</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">At start-up or as it slows to a stop</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Worn bearing, slipping belt, drive belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Listen at each end; check belt tension and drive belt</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            If the sound is closer to a tick than a grind, our <a href="/treadmill-clicking-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill clicking noise</a> guide
            uses the same approach for clicks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="find-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Pin Down Where the Grind Comes From</h2>
<p>
            You are trying to answer two questions: does the grind need your weight on the belt, and which end of the machine
            is it coming from? Do these steps in order and keep a phone recording running with sound, because a short video
            is the single most useful thing to send a support team later.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Clear the area.</strong> Move the treadmill a little away from walls and furniture so you can stand at
            the side and at the back. Remove anything resting on the deck or tucked under it.</li>
            <li><strong>Run it empty, slowly.</strong> Stand beside the treadmill, not on it, with the safety key clipped to
            nothing or held in your hand so you can pull it. Start at the lowest speed and listen at the front (motor cover)
            and at the rear (end cap).</li>
            <li><strong>Raise the speed in steps.</strong> A bearing grind usually gets louder and higher-pitched as the belt
            goes faster. Note whether it tracks the belt speed smoothly.</li>
            <li><strong>Walk on it.</strong> Step on at a slow walking speed. If the grind only appears now, or becomes much
            louder each time your foot lands, the cause is something your weight presses together: the belt and the deck, or
            grit between them.</li>
            <li><strong>Change the incline with nobody on.</strong> If the grind only comes while the deck is rising or
            falling, and stops when it stops, it is the incline system, not the belt drive.</li>
            <li><strong>Listen as it starts and stops.</strong> A grind that is worst in the first seconds, or as the belt
            coasts down, is often a bearing that is drying out or wearing unevenly.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Do not put a hand, a screwdriver or a stethoscope near a moving roller, belt or motor pulley. Every check that
            involves touching parts happens with the treadmill switched off and unplugged.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rollers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Roller Bearings: Front and Rear</h2>
<p>
            The running belt loops around two rollers. The <strong>front roller</strong> is driven by the motor, through the
            drive belt and a pulley on the roller's end. The <strong>rear roller</strong> is an idler that the belt turns,
            and it carries the adjustment bolts you use to tension and centre the belt. Each roller spins on sealed bearings
            at its ends. When a bearing wears, loses its grease or takes in grit, it starts to grind, and the sound rises and
            falls with the belt speed whether or not anyone is walking.
          </p>
          <h3>How to check a roller with the power off</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Unplug the treadmill.</li>
            <li>For the rear roller, remove the end cap if your manual allows it. Look at the roller ends for hair, thread
            or carpet fibre wound around the shaft: that alone can make a rough, scraping noise and is cleared with care and a
            pair of scissors or tweezers.</li>
            <li>Following the manual, slacken the belt tension enough to turn the roller by hand, counting the turns of each
            adjustment bolt so you can put them back exactly.</li>
            <li>Spin the roller. A good bearing turns smoothly and quietly. A worn one feels gritty or notchy, makes a rough
            sound you can hear close up, or lets the roller rock or slide on its shaft.</li>
            <li>Re-tension and re-centre the belt as the manual describes, using the turns you counted.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            The front roller is harder to reach because the drive pulley and motor sit beside it, under the motor cover. If
            your manual does not describe removing that cover, leave it to the maker's service team. A grinding front roller
            and a grinding motor can sound similar from outside the machine; the difference is whether the grind continues
            with the drive belt removed, which is a technician's check on most models.
          </p>
          <p>
            Worn roller bearings are not usually something you can re-grease from outside, because they are sealed. On many
            treadmills the fix is a new roller, or new bearings pressed into the old roller by a technician. A roller that
            grinds tends to get worse, and a bearing that seizes can make the belt drag, slip or overheat the motor, so do
            not run it for months hoping it settles.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Motor Bearings and the Motor Itself</h2>
<p>
            The drive motor sits under the front cover and turns on its own bearings. When those wear, you hear a rough,
            growling grind from the front that follows motor speed. It is there with the belt empty, and it often gets louder
            when the motor is under load. On brushed <a href="/ac-vs-dc-treadmill-motor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">DC motors</a>,
            worn brushes and a dirty commutator make a scratchy, crackling sound that can be mistaken for a grind; see <a href="/treadmill-motor-brushes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill motor brushes</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Signs the motor, not a roller, is the source:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>The grind is loudest right at the motor cover, not at the roller ends.</li>
            <li>It comes with a hot or burning smell, a strained hum, or the <a href="/treadmill-belt-jerking/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>belt surging</a> and slowing.</li>
            <li>The treadmill trips its breaker, shows a motor error on the console, or cuts out under load.</li>
            <li>A technician removes the drive belt and the motor still grinds when it is run on its own.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Dust is the motor's long-term enemy. Fine dust, pet hair and carpet fibre are drawn in by the cooling fan and settle
            around the motor, bearings and electronics. Vacuuming the compartment, with the power off and only if your manual
            says the cover can be removed, is covered in <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to vacuum a treadmill motor compartment</a>.
            A motor that is already grinding will not be cured by cleaning, but cleaning can slow other wear. If the motor
            also hums in a strained way, our <a href="/treadmill-humming-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill humming noise</a> guide explains
            what each kind of hum means.
          </p>
          <p>
            Motor bearings can be replaced on some motors by a repair shop, but on many home treadmills the practical repair
            is a replacement motor. That is the most expensive part in the drive, so it is worth getting a firm diagnosis
            before buying one; see the cost section below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drive-belt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Drive Belt and Pulleys</h2>
<p>
            Between the motor and the front roller is a short drive belt (often a ribbed or toothed belt) running over a pulley
            at each end. It can make a grinding or rasping noise when:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>It is worn or cracked.</strong> A belt with glazed, cracked or missing ribs runs roughly over the
            pulleys and can chatter or grind.</li>
            <li><strong>It is misaligned.</strong> If the motor has shifted on its mount, or a pulley has moved along its
            shaft, the belt rides against the edge of a pulley flange.</li>
            <li><strong>A pulley is loose.</strong> A pulley that is not held firmly by its set screw or key can wobble and
            scrape.</li>
            <li><strong>Debris is in the grooves.</strong> Grit, rubber dust or a small screw caught in the pulley grooves grinds
            with every turn.</li>
            <li><strong>Something touches the flywheel or fan.</strong> A loose wire, a cable tie or a bit of packing material
            under the cover rubs as the motor spins.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            With the treadmill unplugged and the cover off (only if the manual allows), look for black rubber dust around the
            pulleys, a belt sitting off-centre on a pulley, or anything loose near the moving parts. Our <a href="/treadmill-drive-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill drive belt</a> guide covers checking
            and replacing it. Do not confuse the drive belt with the running belt you walk on; tightening the running belt does
            nothing for a drive belt problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="debris" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Debris Under the Running Belt</h2>
<p>
            Grit trapped between the running belt and the deck makes a sandpaper-like grinding that is worst with someone on
            the belt, because your weight presses the grit into both surfaces. Sand from shoes, pet hair, dust and carpet fibre
            are the usual culprits, and a <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>treadmill on carpet</a> or near a door picks up more of them. Left alone, grit wears the
            underside of the belt and the deck surface, and it can work its way into the roller ends.
          </p>
          <p>
            The fix is free: unplug the treadmill, lift the belt edges, and clean the deck surface underneath with a lint-free
            cloth, working along both sides and as far toward the centre as you can reach. Clean the roller ends while you are
            there. Our step-by-step <a href="/how-to-clean-under-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to clean under a treadmill belt</a> guide shows how to do it without disturbing tension and tracking. If cleaning makes the grind quieter but does not
            remove it, the grit may already have scored the deck or the belt, which brings you to the next section.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>A Dry or Worn Deck and Belt</h2>
<p>
            The running belt slides across the deck on a thin film of lubricant. When that film runs out, the belt drags,
            and you hear a rough, scuffing grind with every footstep. The motor also has to work harder to pull the belt, which
            is why a dry deck often comes with a strained motor note, the belt hesitating as your foot lands, or a hot smell.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Check whether lubrication is due.</strong> Your manual gives the interval and the type of lubricant to
            use; many makers specify silicone. Lubricate as it says. See <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt lubrication</a> and <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how often to lubricate a treadmill</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Check that it needs lubricant at all.</strong> Some treadmills use a pre-waxed or low-maintenance belt
            and deck, and adding lubricant they were not designed for can make things worse. The manual is the authority.</li>
            <li><strong>Feel the belt underside.</strong> With the treadmill unplugged, lift the belt edge. A belt that feels
            rough, fuzzy or glazed, or has worn through in patches, will keep grinding even after lubrication.</li>
            <li><strong>Look at the deck.</strong> A deck with a worn track down the middle, or one that has cracked, needs
            attention; see <a href="/treadmill-deck-replacement/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill deck replacement</a> and <a href="/treadmill-cracked-deck/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill cracked deck</a>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A belt and deck wear together, so when one is worn out the other is often close behind. If you are replacing a
            running belt because it grinds, check the deck at the same time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Grinding During Incline Changes</h2>
<p>
            Most powered incline systems use a small motor turning a lift screw or gear that raises the front of the deck.
            Some noise from it is normal while the incline moves. A grind that is new, louder than usual, or comes with the
            incline sticking, stopping short or moving in jerks, points to:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Something trapped under the deck</strong> or in the lift arms, such as a cable, a toy or a sock.</li>
            <li><strong>A dry or dirty lift screw</strong> that has collected dust and hair.</li>
            <li><strong>Worn or stripped gear teeth</strong> in the incline motor or its gearbox.</li>
            <li><strong>A lost incline position</strong> where the machine tries to drive past its limit; calibration usually
            resets this.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Unplug the treadmill and check under and around the lift mechanism first. Then run the <a href="/treadmill-calibration/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline calibration</a> your manual describes.
            If the incline still grinds, sticks or will not move, see <a href="/treadmill-incline-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill incline not working</a>.
            Avoid running the incline up and down repeatedly to test it, because a grinding gear wears faster under repeated
            load.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="start-stop" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Grinding at Start-Up or When It Stops</h2>
<p>
            A grind that is loudest in the first few seconds after you press start, or as the belt coasts to a stop, is a
            useful clue in its own right:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A bearing on its way out.</strong> A worn roller or motor bearing is often noisiest at low speed,
            when the load on it is uneven, and may seem to quieten as speed rises. That does not mean it has recovered.</li>
            <li><strong>A slipping running belt.</strong> If the belt slips on the front roller as it takes up the load, you can
            hear a rubbing or scraping noise and feel a stutter. See <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt slipping</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>The drive belt.</strong> A worn or loose drive belt can chatter as the motor takes up the load.</li>
            <li><strong>Grit settled overnight.</strong> On a treadmill that has sat unused, dust on the deck or roller ends can
            make a brief rough sound that fades as the belt clears it. If it fades in seconds and does not return, cleaning
            under the belt is usually enough.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A clunk or bang as the belt starts or stops is a different problem, usually the belt or a roller catching; our <a href="/treadmill-thumping-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill thumping noise</a> guide covers that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Grinding vs Other Treadmill Noises</h2>
<p>
            People describe the same sound in different ways, so it helps to check you are chasing the right noise. Grinding
            is a continuous, rough, gritty or scraping sound, not a separate tick or knock.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Sound</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What it is like</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Usually points to</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Guide</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Grinding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Continuous, rough, gritty</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bearings, dry deck, grit, gears</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">This page</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Squeaking</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">High, rubbing, often with each step</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dry deck, frame joints, rollers</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700"><a href="/treadmill-squeaking-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Squeaking</a></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Clicking</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Separate, regular ticks</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt seam, loose bolts, drive</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700"><a href="/treadmill-clicking-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Clicking</a></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Thumping</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dull knock, often once per belt lap</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt lump, roller, deck</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700"><a href="/treadmill-thumping-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Thumping</a></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Humming</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Steady electrical drone</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor, fan, electronics</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700"><a href="/treadmill-humming-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Humming</a></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            A squeak that has been ignored can turn into a grind, and so can a roller bearing that started out ticking. If your
            treadmill made a different noise before the grind began, the guide for that noise is a good second place to look.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stop" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>When to Stop Using the Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            A grind is a reason to investigate soon. It is a reason to stop now, unplug, and not use the treadmill again until it
            has been checked, if any of these come with it:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>A burning, hot-rubber or hot-electrical smell; see <a href="/treadmill-burning-smell/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill burning smell</a>.</li>
            <li>Smoke, sparks or visible scorching anywhere on the machine.</li>
            <li>The belt jerking, stalling, surging or slowing as your foot lands.</li>
            <li>The belt not stopping promptly when you pull the safety key.</li>
            <li>The breaker tripping, the machine cutting out, or a motor error on the console.</li>
            <li>A grind that has suddenly become much louder, or a roller that has started to wobble.</li>
            <li>The incline jamming partway, or the deck dropping unevenly.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The risk with a grinding treadmill is not only damage to the machine. A seizing bearing or a stalling motor can make
            the belt stop or jerk under your feet, which is how falls happen. Keep children and pets away and leave it unplugged
            until it is checked. Our <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill troubleshooting</a> guide covers the other symptoms in the order worth checking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Check the Warranty Before Any Repair</h2>
<p>
            Before you open the motor cover or order a part, find out whether the treadmill is still covered. Treadmill
            warranties are usually split by part, with the frame, motor, other parts and labour each covered for a different
            period, so a machine that is out of labour cover may still have a motor or parts warranty running. The terms are
            in your manual, your registration email or on the maker's website.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Find the model and serial number</strong> on the label on the frame, usually near the front or under the
            motor cover.</li>
            <li><strong>Note what you found:</strong> when the grind happens (empty, under load, incline, start/stop), which end
            it comes from, and what you have already tried.</li>
            <li><strong>Send a short video with sound.</strong> It saves a lot of back and forth.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask before you open it.</strong> Some makers want authorized service for certain repairs; check the
            terms before removing covers or fitting non-original parts.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            If support sends a part, they will usually tell you whether it is an owner-fit job or a technician visit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What a Repair Involves</h2>
<p>
            Once the warranty question is settled, the repair depends on the part. In rough order from simplest to most
            involved:
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Cause</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Typical repair</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Who usually does it</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Grit under the belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Clean the deck and roller ends</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Owner</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dry deck</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lubricate per the manual</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Owner</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hair wound on a roller end</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Remove end cap and clear it</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Owner, if the manual allows</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Worn drive belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Replace the drive belt, check pulley alignment</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Handy owner or technician</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Worn running belt or deck</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Replace the belt, and the deck if worn</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Handy owner or technician</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Roller bearing</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Replace the roller, or press in new bearings</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Technician on most models</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline gear or motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Replace the incline motor or gearbox</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Technician on most models</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor bearing or motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Replace bearings or the motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Technician</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            For prices, see our <a href="/treadmill-belt-replacement-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt replacement cost</a> and <a href="/treadmill-motor-replacement-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill motor replacement cost</a> guides, which set out what parts and labour run. On an older budget treadmill, compare the quote with the price of
            a replacement machine before committing, especially if the motor and a roller both need work. If you do replace a
            belt yourself, our <a href="/how-to-replace-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to replace a treadmill belt</a> guide walks through it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-pad" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Grinding on a Walking Pad</h2>
<p>
            Walking pads grind for the same reasons, but the layout changes which causes are most likely. The rollers are small
            and spin faster for a given belt speed, so a worn roller bearing is heard sooner. The motor sits right next to the
            front roller under a low cover, so front-roller and motor noises are hard to tell apart from outside. And because
            pads are often slid under sofas and beds, they collect dust and hair at the roller ends quickly.
          </p>
          <p>
            Start by cleaning the roller ends and under the belt, then lubricate if the manual says to. Many pads do not have an
            owner-removable motor cover, so a grind from the front that survives cleaning is usually a support call. Our <a href="/walking-pad-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad not working</a> guide covers belt
            tension and centring on pads.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prevent" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Keep It From Coming Back</h2>
<ul>
            <li><strong>Keep grit out.</strong> Wear clean shoes used only indoors, vacuum around the treadmill regularly, and
            use a mat under it to keep floor dust and carpet fibre out of the rollers and motor.</li>
            <li><strong>Lubricate on the maker's schedule,</strong> with the lubricant the manual names, and not more often than
            it says.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the belt centred and correctly tensioned.</strong> A belt that runs to one side loads one roller
            bearing harder; an over-tight belt loads both rollers and the motor bearings. See <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to center a treadmill belt</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Stay within the user weight limit.</strong> Heavier loads than the rating put more strain on the deck,
            rollers and motor.</li>
            <li><strong>Act on new noises early.</strong> A bearing that has just started to grind is a planned repair; one that
            seizes is an emergency. Our <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill maintenance</a> guide sets out a routine.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Goes Wrong With a Grinding Treadmill</h2>
          <p>A grind rarely fixes itself. These make it slower or more expensive to sort out.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Lubricating a bearing problem</h3>
              <p>Lubricant helps a dry deck, not a worn roller or motor bearing. If the grind is there with nobody on the belt, more lubricant will not stop it and too much can make the belt slip.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Tightening the belt to quiet it</h3>
              <p>An over-tight running belt loads the roller and motor bearings harder and can make a bearing grind worse. Adjust tension only to the manual and only for slipping.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Opening covers before checking the warranty</h3>
              <p>Some makers want certain repairs done by authorized service. Find the serial number and ask support first, with a video, before you remove covers or fit parts.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Running it until it seizes</h3>
              <p>A grind that is growing will not settle. A bearing that seizes can stall the belt under you and damage the motor, turning a roller job into a bigger repair.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The fix, in short</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Find out when it grinds: with nobody on, only under load, during incline changes, or at
            start and stop.</strong> Empty-belt grinding that rises with speed is usually a roller or motor bearing; load-only
            grinding is usually a dry deck or grit, which cleaning and lubricating can fix.
          </p>
          <p>
            Stop using the treadmill if the grind comes with a burning smell, a jerking or stalling belt, or tripping, and check
            the warranty before opening it up or buying parts. Our <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill troubleshooting guide</a> covers every other symptom.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="ed-more not-prose">
          <div class="ed-more-k">Keep reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/treadmill-clicking-noise/">Treadmill Clicking Noise</a></li>
            <li><a href="/treadmill-humming-noise/">Treadmill Humming Noise</a></li>
          </ul>
        </aside>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Why is my treadmill making a grinding noise?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually a worn roller or motor bearing (heard with nobody on, rising with speed), a dry or worn deck or grit under the belt (heard only when walking), or the incline gear (heard only while the incline moves). Note when it happens to narrow it down.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it safe to use a treadmill that is grinding?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not for long. Investigate a mild grind soon, and stop immediately if it comes with a burning smell, a jerking or stalling belt, tripping, or a sudden increase in noise. A seizing bearing or stalling motor can stop the belt under your feet.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I know if my treadmill roller bearing is bad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              With the treadmill unplugged and the belt slackened as the manual describes, spin the roller by hand. A good bearing turns smoothly and quietly; a bad one feels gritty or notchy, sounds rough close up, or lets the roller rock on its shaft.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can lubricating the belt stop a grinding noise?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, if the grind only happens when someone is walking and the deck is due for lubricant. It will not fix a worn bearing, drive belt or motor, and some belts are pre-waxed and should not be lubricated, so check the manual first.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why does my treadmill grind when the incline changes?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The incline motor, its lift screw or gear, or something trapped under the deck. Unplug, check under the deck, then run the incline calibration in your manual. If it still grinds or sticks, the incline motor or gearbox may need replacing.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a grinding treadmill motor worth repairing?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On a treadmill under its motor warranty, yes: contact the maker first. Out of warranty, compare a replacement motor quote with the cost of a new treadmill; see our motor replacement cost guide for typical prices.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
          <div class="author-card">
            <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="56" height="56" loading="lazy" />
            <div>
              <div class="ac-k">Written by</div>
              <div class="ac-n"><a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a></div>
              <p class="ac-b">
                Exercise Physiology &amp; Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS. PhD in Kinesiology
                (Biomechanics), University of Michigan. His evaluations look at motor performance, belt
                stability, shock absorption, incline accuracy, noise and long-term durability. <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">LinkedIn</a>
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

      </div>
